WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.172 --> 00:00:02.130
ANTHONY BUSHONG: When
getting started with GKE,

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.980
it's essential to think about
how you can design for security

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:06.840
across multiple dimensions.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.090
Today, let's review
the first areas

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:11.040
you'll need to consider
when securing access

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:11.985
to your GKE cluster.

00:00:11.985 --> 00:00:15.150
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.458 --> 00:00:21.000
It's important to
start with defining

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.340
what cluster access means.

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.320
With GKE, there
are two dimensions

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:27.520
in which we access our cluster.

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.730
The first dimension
is the GKE API.

00:00:30.730 --> 00:00:34.260
This is where users can
perform cluster operations.

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.480
Rather than creating
Kubernetes resources,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.100
we use the GKE API to create,
read, update, and delete

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:43.650
GKE clusters.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:46.680
The second dimension is the
Kubernetes control plane.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:48.870
This is how we
create, read, update,

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:53.760
and delete Kubernetes
resources in a GKE cluster.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.450
Across both the GKE API and
the Kubernetes control plane,

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:01.590
we want to ensure that users
only have the access they need

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.460
and nothing more.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.600
This is called the principle
of least privilege.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.750
Let's review the various
knobs we have available in GKE

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.160
to develop a strategy
to achieve this.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:17.760
Accessing a GKE cluster
starts with GCP Identity

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.120
and Access Management, or IAM.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.360
Like most GCP services,
GKE is tightly integrated

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:26.700
with GCP IAM.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:28.800
In GKE, roles and
permissions can

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.400
be assigned to individual users
or an entire Google Group.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.250
This can help greatly as the
surface of your team changes

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.265
over time.

00:01:36.265 --> 00:01:37.890
One of the quickest
ways to get started

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:42.840
with accessing GKE is using
GCP IAM's predefined roles.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.580
In GKE, there are
predefined roles,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.520
such as admin, developer,
viewer, all of which

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.010
can be assigned to user
accounts or a Google Group

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.470
using GCP IAM.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:57.060
It is important to note that
the role will apply to all GKE

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.600
clusters in a GCP project.

00:02:00.600 --> 00:02:02.790
Once assigned a
predefined role, users

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:05.880
will have a set of permissions
that authorize them

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.729
to interact with the GKE API.

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:10.603
This means that they
can perform operations

00:02:10.603 --> 00:02:12.270
that align with their
role, whether it's

00:02:12.270 --> 00:02:14.580
creating a cluster,
adding a node pool,

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.880
or manually triggering
a cluster upgrade.

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.310
These predefined
roles also give users

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:23.550
authorization to perform
various actions when interacting

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:25.890
with the Kubernetes API.

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:28.440
Again, it is important to
note that not only does this

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:30.390
apply to all clusters
in a project,

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:34.260
but all namespaces
within a GKE cluster.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.820
Now this can be
really convenient,

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:38.070
especially if you're a team
doing your own development

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:40.170
and your own operations.

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.570
GKE's integration
with GCP IAM can

00:02:42.570 --> 00:02:44.790
be a single method
for configuring access

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.910
to both the GKE API and
Kubernetes resources

00:02:47.910 --> 00:02:50.440
within your GKE clusters.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.560
However, if we need
more granular control

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.900
to achieve the principle
of least privilege,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.360
we'll have to be a bit more
intentional, configuring

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.480
GCP IAM and Kubernetes
Role-Based Access

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.530
Control, or RBAC, in
concert with one another.

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:10.230
We can start by looking at
creating a custom GCP IAM role.

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:12.510
Rather than utilizing
the predefined roles

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.850
that GKE provides,
we can consider

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:19.260
creating a custom GCP IAM
role with lesser permission.

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:22.800
We can then use Kubernetes
RBAC to define user access when

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.420
interacting with the Kubernetes
control plane in a GKE cluster.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:30.510
Kubernetes RBAC allows us
to enable granular control

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:33.420
over what namespaces and
what Kubernetes resources

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.660
that users can interact
with on a per-cluster basis.

00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.240
An example of this
in action would

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:42.990
be to define a GCP IAM
custom role for users

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.780
that don't need to manage
the GKE cluster lifecycle,

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:48.180
but do need to interact
with the Kubernetes control

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.990
plane for various
clusters in their project.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.330
In this scenario,
the custom role

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.970
would be minimal,
only allowing the user

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.700
get permissions with
the GKE API, which

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:02.490
enables them to get their
credentials to access

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.230
the Kubernetes control plane.

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:06.390
But by default, this
would not give them

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.640
permissions to actually
perform any actions

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.490
against the Kubernetes
control plane.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.670
Instead any permissions when
interacting with the Kubernetes

00:04:14.670 --> 00:04:18.690
control plane would be defined
in Kubernetes RBAC, which

00:04:18.690 --> 00:04:20.790
can give us the
granularity we need

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:23.410
to achieve the principle
of least privilege.

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:27.120
Now once we have found the right
balance of our GCP IAM roles

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:30.150
and utilizing
Kubernetes RBAC, GKE

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:32.700
provides another
layer for us to secure

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:38.040
cluster access, this time around
a user's network identity.

00:04:38.040 --> 00:04:41.820
Even if a user is already setup
with GCP IAM and Kubernetes

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:45.450
RBAC correctly, we may also
want to verify the network

00:04:45.450 --> 00:04:47.790
from which they are running.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:50.940
In GKE, authorized
networks is a feature

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.790
that allows us to only
enable communication

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:56.910
with the Kubernetes control
plane in our GKE clusters

00:04:56.910 --> 00:05:00.390
from a trusted set
of IP addresses.

00:05:00.390 --> 00:05:02.550
This adds an additional
layer of security

00:05:02.550 --> 00:05:04.710
to not only ensure that
our user is authorized

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:06.510
to perform a certain
set of actions,

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.510
but is also performing these
actions from a trusted network

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:10.290
perimeter.

00:05:10.290 --> 00:05:14.910
To recap, configuring
GCP IAM, Kubernetes RBAC,

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:17.760
and GKE authorized
networks is a great place

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:20.310
to start when building
a GKE cluster that

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:24.738
adheres to your organization's
security best practices.

00:05:24.738 --> 00:05:26.280
Now that we've
covered the essentials

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.560
for securing cluster
access in GKE,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.410
visit the links
here to learn more.

00:05:31.410 --> 00:05:33.498
With the concepts
discussed in this video,

00:05:33.498 --> 00:05:35.040
we're confident
you'll be on your way

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:38.370
to building a GKE cluster
that suits your needs.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:44.090
Stay tuned as we expand on other
GKE topics in "GKE Essentials."

